---
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import { CORE_COMMIT_SHORT, CORE_RELEASE_TAG, CORE_REPO, PYPI_URL, milestone, roadmapSnapshot, specs, versions } from '../data/facts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const snapshotUrl = `${CORE_REPO}/commit/${roadmapSnapshot.commit}`;
const snapshotShort = roadmapSnapshot.commit.slice(0, 7);
// File links stay pinned to the release (CORE_COMMIT), as everywhere on the
// site; the board below is newer than the roadmap file at that release.
const languageRoadmap = specs.languageRoadmap;
const evolutionRules = specs.languageEvolutionRules;

// The language iterations, read from docs/LANGUAGE_ROADMAP.md at the snapshot
// commit. One gap per iteration; each runs L1 to L6 and ends in a release.
// state: ok = released on PyPI, exp = tagged or in progress, wait = not started.
const iterations = [
  { code: 'I1', gap: 'G-PATH', direction: 'Safety', adds: 'Region limits: stay inside or outside a region while moving', language: '1.7', state: 'ok', status: 'Departed', when: '0.4.0 · 2026-11-17' },
  { code: 'I2', gap: 'G-ACT', direction: 'Action', adds: 'Declared actions: open, set a device, print, from the robot profile', language: '1.8', state: 'ok', status: 'Departed', when: '0.5.0 · 2026-11-17' },
  { code: 'I3', gap: 'G-CLOCK', direction: 'Reasoning', adds: 'Clock readings and times of day in conditions', language: '1.9', state: 'ok', status: 'Departed', when: '0.6.0 · 2026-11-17' },
  { code: 'I4', gap: 'G-LOOP', direction: 'Reasoning', adds: 'Missions: bounded, verified repetition with a stop condition', language: '2.0', state: 'ok', status: 'Departed', when: '0.7.0 · 2026-11-18' },
  { code: 'I5', gap: 'G-MSG', direction: 'Communication', adds: 'Waiting for, and checking, an incoming message', language: '2.1', state: 'ok', status: 'Departed', when: '0.8.0 · 2026-11-18' },
  { code: 'I6', gap: 'G-PERCEIVE', direction: 'Perception', adds: 'More measured kinds: fill, confidence, pressure, count', language: '2.2', state: 'exp', status: 'Tagged', when: '0.9.0 · PyPI pending' },
  { code: 'I7', gap: 'G-CHOOSE', direction: 'Perception', adds: 'Properties of robots and locations; choosing the nearest or best', language: '2.3', state: 'run', status: 'Boarding · L3', when: 'L4 build next' },
  { code: 'I8', gap: 'G-SELF', direction: 'Autonomy', adds: "The robot's battery as a reading and condition", language: '2.4', state: 'wait', status: 'Scheduled', when: '—' },
  { code: 'I9', gap: 'G-CALC', direction: 'Reasoning', adds: 'Minimal arithmetic, counting and text built from values', language: '2.5', state: 'wait', status: 'Scheduled', when: '—' },
  { code: 'I10', gap: 'G-CONC', direction: 'Cooperation', adds: 'Several robots acting at the same time', language: '2.6', state: 'wait', status: 'Scheduled', when: '—' },
  { code: 'I11+', gap: 'I11 to I20', direction: 'Several', adds: 'People nearby, force limits, triggers, shifts, teaching, quality, categories, prediction, negotiation, shared spaces', language: '2.7 to 3.0', state: 'off', status: 'Proposed', when: '—' },
];

const cycle = [
  { step: 'L1', name: 'Choose', text: 'The top gap from the generated order, verified by probe tasks.' },
  { step: 'L2', name: 'Design', text: 'A one-page decision record: the smallest construct and what it must not rule out.' },
  { step: 'L3', name: 'Pre-register', text: "The gap's benchmark tasks and acceptance bounds, frozen before any code." },
  { step: 'L4', name: 'Build', text: 'Specification, grammar, validator, simulation, Task IR, reference and examples.' },
  { step: 'L5', name: 'Accept', text: 'The tasks pass, and false programs and decision accuracy are no worse.' },
  { step: 'L6', name: 'Release', text: 'A new version, the gap closed in the activity atlas, a log entry.' },
];
---

<Base
  title="Vision & Roadmap"
  description="RoboLang's research direction, how the language evolves one gap at a time, its development status on 24 November 2026 and the horizons it is working through. Nothing on this page runs on a robot."
>
  <PageHead slot="head" index="05" label="Vision & Roadmap" title="Vision & roadmap">
    Where RoboLang is trying to go, and how that differs from what you can install today.
  </PageHead>

  <div class="prose">
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>Plans and features are marked apart.</strong> Stages marked Released are in the
        package on PyPI; stages marked Delivered are in the core repository but not yet on PyPI.
        Nothing on this page runs on a robot, enforces a safety limit or includes an AI model, and
        plans may change.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Vision</h2>
    <p>
      The research question behind RoboLang is whether one small, precisely specified task language
      can sit between the people and AI systems that decide what a robot should do and the varied
      software that makes robots move.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Interoperable tasks.</strong> The same task description, checked once, understood by
        different robots through adapters that declare what each robot can do.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Multi-robot communication.</strong> Structured, versioned messages for handing work
        from one robot to another, in place of free text.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI-generated programs.</strong> A language strict enough that a task written by a
        language model can be rejected with a precise reason before it reaches anything physical.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The project builds toward this in order: a correct language first, then execution in simulation,
      then interoperability, then the case for hardware. Each stage is meant to be verifiable before
      the next begins.
    </p>

    <h2 id="evolution">How the language evolves</h2>
    <p>
      Since 16 November 2026 new language features come from the robot activities expected within three
      years, collected in an activity atlas with scored forecasts. Each missing capability is a
      <strong>gap</strong>. A gap gets syntax only when benchmark tasks prove that today's language
      cannot express it, and the order of the gaps is generated from the atlas, not chosen by hand
      (<a href={evolutionRules}>rules</a>, <a href={languageRoadmap}>language roadmap</a> as of
      release <code>{CORE_RELEASE_TAG}</code>; the board below is newer).
    </p>
    <p>
      One gap per <strong>iteration</strong>. Each iteration runs the same six steps, each approved by
      the project owner, and ends in a release:
    </p>
  </div>

  <ol class="path path--six">
    {
      cycle.map((c, i) => (
        <li>
          <span class={i < 3 ? 'dot dot--done' : 'dot'} aria-hidden="true">{c.step}</span>
          <p class="syslabel">{c.step}</p>
          <h3>{c.name}</h3>
          <p>{c.text}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <div class="board">
    <div class="board-head">
      <h3 class="wide">Language departures · iterations I1 to I20</h3>
      <p class="syslabel">Read {roadmapSnapshot.date} · core <a href={snapshotUrl}><code>{snapshotShort}</code></a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Language iterations">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Iteration</th>
            <th scope="col">Gap</th>
            <th scope="col">Direction</th>
            <th scope="col">What it adds</th>
            <th scope="col">Language</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
            <th scope="col">Release</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            iterations.map((it) => (
              <tr class={it.state === 'run' ? 'goal-row' : undefined}>
                <td>
                  <span class="flap" aria-hidden="true">{[...it.code].map((c) => <i>{c}</i>)}</span>
                  <span class="sr-only">{it.code}</span>
                </td>
                <th scope="row">{it.gap}</th>
                <td>{it.direction}</td>
                <td class="wrap-cell">{it.adds}</td>
                <td>{it.language}</td>
                <td><span class={`light light--${it.state}`}>{it.status}</span></td>
                <td class="dimlabel">{it.when}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="board-foot">
      <span>
        Departed: on PyPI · Tagged: released on GitHub, PyPI upload pending · Boarding: in progress ·
        I11 to I20 are proposals until the research confirms them
      </span>
      <a class="signal" href={languageRoadmap}>Language roadmap →</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      <strong>Where I7 stands.</strong> Decision record 0045, choosing at run time, was
      <a href={roadmapSnapshot.decision0045}>approved</a> on 18 November 2026: choosing by position and
      straight-line distance, <code>delegate</code> accepting choices made before the first action, and
      choices inside missions. Its
      benchmark set 12 "choose" is being pre-registered (L3). The build of RoboLang 2.3 (L4) starts
      once the owner freezes that set. Nothing of 2.3 is implemented yet.
    </p>

    <h2 id="status">Development status, {roadmapSnapshot.date}</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>A dated snapshot.</strong> The statuses below were read from the core repository at
        commit <a href={snapshotUrl}><code>{snapshotShort}</code></a> on {roadmapSnapshot.date}. The
        command examples and technical facts elsewhere on this site stay pinned to release
        <code>{CORE_RELEASE_TAG}</code> (core commit <code>{CORE_COMMIT_SHORT}</code>), which is what
        <code>pip install robolang</code> installs and what they were captured and verified against.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Released</strong> means in a distribution on <a href={PYPI_URL}>PyPI</a>, the latest
        being {versions.distribution}. <strong>Delivered</strong> means merged into the core repository
        but not on PyPI. <strong>Approved</strong> means a decision is recorded; it does not authorize
        implementation. <strong>Planned</strong> means the work has not started.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Released, up to {versions.distribution}</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Stage</th>
            <th scope="col">Focus</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Language 1.1 to 1.4</th>
            <td>
              Parser, static validation and versioned IR; combined conditions (1.2); goals that decide
              success and typed sensors (1.3); bounded branching and <code>validate --world</code> (1.4).
              Symbolic simulation with a deterministic run record, <code>export-goal</code>,
              <code>delegate</code> and the Core 0.1 reference simulation.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Released</strong> in 0.2.0, the first PyPI release, 2026-10-30. Foundation 1
              (<a href={specs.foundation1Evidence}>evidence</a>) and gate 1
              (<a href={specs.gate1Evidence}>evidence</a>) signed off 2026-10-27
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={roadmapSnapshot.release030}>Language 1.5 and 1.6, Core 0.2</a></th>
            <td>
              Step failure handlers (1.5) and measured values (1.6), each accepted on its own
              pre-registered benchmark set. Core 0.2, frozen 2026-10-30, opt-in with
              <code>--core 0.2</code>.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Released</strong> in 0.3.0, 2026-11-11</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Language 1.7 to 2.1</th>
            <td>
              Iterations I1 to I5 above: region limits, declared actions, clock readings, the mission
              layer and incoming messages. Also the physical-run authorization documents and optional
              Ed25519 signatures (<code>robolang[ed25519]</code>); physical mode stays disabled
              (<code>A731</code>).
            </td>
            <td><strong>Released</strong> in 0.4.0 to {versions.distribution}, 2026-11-17 to 2026-11-18</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>In the core repository, not on PyPI</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Stage</th>
            <th scope="col">Focus</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={roadmapSnapshot.lang22}>Language {roadmapSnapshot.pendingLanguage}</a></th>
            <td>
              More measured kinds: <code>fill</code> and <code>confidence</code> in <code>%</code>,
              <code>pressure</code> and <code>count</code> (iteration I6).
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Delivered</strong> and <a href={roadmapSnapshot.release090}>accepted</a>
              2026-11-18, tagged <code>v{roadmapSnapshot.pendingRelease}</code>. The PyPI upload waits for
              the owner's approval
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={roadmapSnapshot.decision0045Draft}>Language 2.3</a></th>
            <td>Choosing robots and locations at run time (iteration I7).</td>
            <td>Decision <strong>approved</strong> 2026-11-18; benchmark set being pre-registered (L3). Not built</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={roadmapSnapshot.h3Acceptance}>ROS 2 backend in physics simulation</a></th>
            <td>
              The same <code>.rob</code> tasks and goals run through ROS 2 and MoveIt in Gazebo, with
              goals verified from camera, TF and gripper observations instead of simulator ground
              truth. Since 2026-11-12 also RoboLang's own stop and safe state. A separate, optional
              package.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Delivered</strong>, physics simulation only. Acceptance 2026-10-31: 33/33 runs
              succeeded, 51/51 goal checks agreed with an independent oracle, no false success
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={roadmapSnapshot.g1Proposal}>Humanoid backend (Unitree G1)</a></th>
            <td>
              A Unitree G1 humanoid in the MuJoCo simulator, behind Unitree's SDK: walking, grasp and
              place, and goal verdicts from the robot's own observations (decision 0046).
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Delivered</strong>, simulation only. Phases 1 to 3
              (<a href={roadmapSnapshot.g1Phase3}>phase 3</a>) done 2026-11-19 to 2026-11-22
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={roadmapSnapshot.heldout}>Held-out benchmark set</a></th>
            <td>
              At least 50 plain-English requests written by people outside the project, kept private and
              run only at milestones, so no prompt or model is tuned on them.
            </td>
            <td>Rules and writers' brief merged 2026-11-22. Set not yet frozen</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Physical robots</th>
            <td>Running tasks on real hardware, through adapters and local safety controls.</td>
            <td>Not started and not authorized</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="horizons">Horizons</h2>
    <p>
      The project works through four horizons, set in its <a href={specs.strategy}>strategy</a>,
      which the owner approved on 11 November 2026. A horizon ends when its exit criteria hold, not
      on a date.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Horizon</th>
            <th scope="col">Exit criteria</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">H1 Trust the basics</th>
            <td>
              <code>pip install robolang</code> works; the language reference ships and is tested; AI
              models make the right write-or-decline decision on at least 97% of benchmark tasks.
            </td>
            <td>
              Install and reference met. Decision accuracy 97.5 to 100% for every model in single runs;
              the three-repeat run that decides it is not yet recorded
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">H2 Express real requests</th>
            <td>
              Branching goal correctness at least 90%; false programs on inexpressible tasks at most
              5%, even with the grammar only; Core 0.2 frozen.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Met</strong> 2026-10-30</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">H3 Leave the symbolic world</th>
            <td>
              One benchmark task family passes end to end in physics simulation, verified through
              sensor observations; an independent Core 0.2 implementation interoperates (Gate 2).
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Met</strong> {milestone.date}. An independent Core 0.2 implementation, written
              clean-room from the frozen bundle alone, passed all 25 Gate 2 cases
              (<a href={roadmapSnapshot.gate2Runs}>runs</a>, <a href={specs.gate2Evidence}>evidence</a>)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">H4 Earn hardware</th>
            <td>
              A written safety case and owner authorization for physical mode. Never before H3 is
              met.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Current horizon</strong>, started 2026-11-12: a robot-independent
              <a href={roadmapSnapshot.safetyCase}>safety case draft</a> and hazard list, and the
              <a href={roadmapSnapshot.decision0038}>physical-mode authorization design</a> built in
              simulation. Physical mode is not authorized
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Language versions ({versions.language} released, {roadmapSnapshot.pendingLanguage} tagged) name the
      syntax of <code>.rob</code> files and are separate from the milestones above, and both are
      separate from the distribution version of the Python package; see
      <a href="/get-started/#versions">the version numbers</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full plan, with the scope and exit criteria for each stage, is
      the <a href={specs.developmentPlan}>development plan</a> in the core repository, linked as it was at
      release <code>{CORE_RELEASE_TAG}</code>; the status snapshot above is newer. The architecture
      the stages build toward is <a href={specs.rfc001}>RFC-001</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Physical robots</h2>
    <p>
      Running RoboLang against real hardware is outside the current scope of the project. The supported
      package cannot do it: its simulations are symbolic and run in one process. The ROS 2 and
      humanoid backends run only in physics simulators and are not authorized for hardware; physical
      mode needs a written safety case and an explicit owner decision first. The older prototype code
      in the repository has never been validated against a robot. Please do not connect RoboLang to
      physical equipment.
    </p>

    <h2>Help shape it</h2>
    <p>
      The most useful input at this stage is on the language and the contracts: what is awkward to
      express, which diagnostics are unclear, what a consumer of the JSON IR or a run record needs, and
      where Core {versions.coreDefault} is ambiguous. See <a href="/contribute/">Contribute</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
